## Goal
Assess how the six treated and untreated RNA-seq samples separate in a PCA of gene-level counts. Show the samples colored and labeled by condition, and report the variance explained by the first two principal components.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | `01_prepare_samplesheets` | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
1. In a Python script using the workspace's `gars-bio` environment, read the resolved tab-separated gene counts and design CSV without modifying either input. Match count columns to `sample_id` values and fail clearly if sample IDs do not match.
2. Use the gene-level length-scaled counts as input. Exclude genes with zero counts across all six samples; convert counts to counts per million (CPM), apply `log2(CPM + 1)`, and run PCA on genes as features and samples as observations after centering and scaling each gene across samples. Do not use treatment labels in fitting the PCA.
3. Write a PC1 vs PC2 scatter plot to `results/sample_pca.png`, with points labeled by sample ID and colored by condition, and include the percentage of variance explained in each axis label. Write the sample coordinates, sample ID, condition, and variance explained for PC1 and PC2 to `results/pca_coordinates.csv`.
4. Run the script through the configured batch executor. The analysis reads upstream artifacts only and writes outputs only under this analysis directory.

## Outputs
